<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">William Clito</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1102–<a href="/years/1128">1128</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">William Clito was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a>, duke of Normandy, and a claimant to Normandy against his uncle Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. After the murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-good">Charles the Good</a> in 1127, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vi-of-france">Louis VI of France</a> imposed him as count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>, but his demands soon turned the towns against him and they backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thierry-of-alsace">Thierry of Alsace</a>. He died of a wound at the siege of Aalst in 1128.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/william-clito.jpg" alt="William Clito" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_William_Clito.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
